parts: C = parts.insert.heatset ('data')
parts: C = parts.insert.heatset ('data', NAME)
parts: C = parts.insert.heatset ('data', NAME, L)
parts: S = parts.insert.heatset ('solid', NAME, L)
parts: ... = parts.insert.heatset (..., Name, Value)
Heat-set threaded inserts: knurled brass inserts with a metric thread,
pressed hot into a hole in a 3D-printed or other thermoplastic part.

C = parts.insert.heatset ('data') returns every insert
in the catalogue as a table, one row for each insert and maker that sells
it, sorted by thread, length and outside diameter.
C = parts.insert.heatset ('data', NAME) returns
those of the thread NAME, as 'M4', and
C = parts.insert.heatset ('data', NAME, L)
those L millimetres long, each with the hole it needs. Nothing is
built.

S = parts.insert.heatset ('solid', NAME, L)
builds the insert as a solid.Shape: its top face, where the screw
enters, on the xy plane, the insert below it to z =
-L, its axis on the z axis. Placed at the mouth of a hole
drilled along -z, it sits flush with the surface, as it is set.
S.Name holds the designation, as
'Heat-set insert M4 x 8.1'. Only inserts in the catalogue are
built, to ruthex's dimensions where ruthex sells the insert, else to CNC
Kitchen's.

No function cuts or sizes the hole, here or in drafting: a printed
hole comes out a different size with each printer, material and setting,
so the designer makes it. A row's d3 is its maker's recommendation
to start from; its t and w are the depth and wall the part
must leave for the insert.

Name/Value pairs:

'Maker'
For 'data': only that maker's rows, 'ruthex' or
'CNC Kitchen', in any case.
'Diameter'
Only the insert of that outside diameter d1. Two inserts are M3 x
4, one 4.6 and one 5 across, the second made for the Voron printers, and a
solid of either needs it.
'Detailed'
For 'solid': true knurls the two bands, chamfers the end
of the pilot and cuts the thread through by solid.Shape.thread. By
default (false) the insert has its outline, the bands plain at
d1, a groove after each and the pilot, with a bore of the thread's
nominal diameter.

The table has these variables, each with its unit and its description,
which summary (C) lists; lengths are in millimetres:

designation thread by length, as 'M4 x 8.1',
and by outside diameter between them where two inserts share both, as
'M3 x 5 x 4'
thread thread, as 'M4'
d nominal diameter of the thread
L length, nominal
d1 outside diameter, over the knurl
d2 diameter of the pilot at the lower end; NaN
where the maker gives none
d3 diameter of the hole the maker recommends
t depth of a blind hole, smallest: L + 1
w wall round the hole, smallest
maker maker
article maker's article number; empty where the maker's
document gives none
source document the insert was read from

The form

The makers' inserts differ only in their knurls, which no fit depends on,
so every insert is built to one form from its own L,
d1 and d2, in proportions read from the makers' models. This
form is a modelling choice; no maker dimensions it. Where no maker gives
d2, the pilot is 0.1 smaller than the hole, as ruthex's are. With
h = (d1 - d2) / 2, from the top down:

Feature From the top Diameter
upper band 0 to 0.29 L d1, knurled
left-hand at 50 degrees
groove 0.29 L to 0.40 L d2 - 0.75
h
lower band 0.40 L to 0.75 L d1,
knurled right-hand at 45 degrees
groove 0.75 L to 0.85 L d2 - 1.25
h
pilot 0.85 L to L d2, chamfered
L/50 at 45 degrees at its end when detailed

The bands are knurled only when detailed. The knurls have the teeth of
90 degrees of DIN 82, cut to full depth with their roots at d2: a
pitch of 2 h, fitted by solid.Shape.knurl to a whole number
of teeth.

The catalogue

Every insert ruthex or CNC Kitchen sells with a metric thread, a row for
each maker with its own values. Where both sell an insert they agree,
except that CNC Kitchen recommends holes 0.1 larger from M4 to M8, gives
M8 x 12.7 an outside diameter of 10.2 against 10.1, and gives the M3 x 5
x 4 a wall of 1.6 against 1.3.

ruthex: the RX series product data sheet, metric ISO thread, status
15.08.2022, page 1, dimensions and order numbers, for every insert but two
sold since, read from the drawings and item numbers on their packets as
ruthex.de shows them: M6 x 6.8 (RX-M6Sx6.8) and M10 x 12.7
(RX-M10x12.7).

CNC Kitchen: Dimensions & Design Guidelines, metric inserts, June
2025. It gives no pilot diameter and no article numbers.

The depth of a blind hole, L + 1, is drawn by both. The proportions
of the form come from the makers' own published STEP models,
measured: ruthex's ten metric models and CNC Kitchen's twelve.

