Properties of the materials parts are made of.

M = parts.material () returns every material in the catalogue
as a table, one row for each material and range of section sizes. A
metal's strength depends on the size of the section, the diameter of a bar
or the thickness of a plate, so its source tabulates it by ranges, a row
each; a polymer's does not, and it has one row. M =
parts.material (NAME) returns the rows of the one named, such as
'C45', and M = parts.material (NAME,
SIZE) the one row for a section SIZE millimetres across.

The table has these variables, each with its unit and its description,
which summary (M) lists; stresses are in megapascals:

name name it is asked for by
class 'metal', 'machined polymer' or
'printed polymer'
section sizes of section the row holds for, over the
first up to the second, two columns; 0 to Inf for a polymer
E Young's modulus
nu Poisson's ratio
Rp yield strength, 0.2 per cent proof stress or tensile
stress at yield; NaN where the source gives none
Rm tensile strength; NaN where the source gives
none
A elongation at break, per cent; NaN where the
source gives none
rho density, kilograms per cubic metre
source where the values come from

Values are minimums where the source states minimums. A printed polymer
carries the weaker of the orientations its datasheet gives, since a printed
part is loaded across its layers somewhere.

